KaiT2en-NixOS divergence: ask a receiver that changed its port, say why
one did not answer, and stop retrying when retrying cannot help.

An Apple device advertises a different AirDrop port depending on what it
is doing: macOS receives on 8770, and both macOS and iOS advertise a
high port while they browse for receivers of their own. Only the
receiver-mode advertisement answers /Discover. Observed on a MacBook Pro
16,1 against an iPhone 15 over four hours -- the same phone, one MAC:

  17:54 port 58988   18:03 port 58990   19:02 port 8770  <- answered
  18:14 port 58991   18:54 port 58993   19:35 port 8770
  19:05 port 58996   19:14 port 58998   19:43 port 8770
  19:21 port 59000   20:53 port 49784   21:26 port 8770

Three failures compounded, and together they made sending look broken
while receiving worked:

1. `asked` was keyed on the MAC alone, so the browse-mode port was asked
   once and the receiver-mode port that followed was never asked at all.
   It is keyed on the port too now, so a device that switches into
   receiver mode is asked again at once.

2. A receiver that answers nothing held its MAC in `asked` for the whole
   DISCOVER_ATTEMPTS budget -- 20 attempts of read timeout, about five
   minutes -- so the "try again when it shows up the next time" path on a
   failed discover could not run inside a browse that lasts a minute.
   Such a receiver is reachable and chose not to serve the request, which
   retrying does not change, so this ends the loop after the first one,
   with a message that names what happened.

3. AWDL taken down under the loop. housekeeping() drops awdl0 as "not
   needed" as soon as the browser leaves, while the discover threads of
   the peers it found are still running. Their peer address keeps the
   scope id of the interface that is gone, so every further attempt fails
   at once with ENETUNREACH and the budget burns in a few seconds:

     18:57:19 ... 18:57:32  waiting for 3a:54:76:5b:84:3d (~13 attempts)
     18:57:32  discover: Network is unreachable (os error 101)
     18:57:58  AWDL up                     -- too late, budget spent

   ENETUNREACH now ends the loop with a message that says so. Only
   ENETUNREACH: the EADDRNOTAVAIL of an address still running duplicate
   address detection is what the retry loop exists for and must keep
   being retried.

The CLI message for an empty browse no longer asserts the receiver's
AirDrop setting as the cause -- it is only one of several -- and points
at the service log, which now carries the reason.

Applied by apps/t2-drop/package.nix on top of the upstream source
(-p1 relative to apps/t2-drop).

Drop condition: remove when upstream keys the asked set by port, keeps
awdl0 up while discover threads are outstanding, and distinguishes a read
timeout from a connect failure. Re-check every sync.

